<h2>1968–71: the 13-character layout</h2>
<div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6"><table class="w-full text-sm text-left text-silver-300 border border-white/10"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-silver-200"><tr><th class="p-3">Position</th><th class="p-3">Example</th><th class="p-3">Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">1</td><td class="p-3">1</td><td class="p-3">Chevrolet</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">2–3</td><td class="p-3">94</td><td class="p-3">Corvette (V8)</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">4–5</td><td class="p-3">37</td><td class="p-3">37 = coupe, 67 = convertible</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">6</td><td class="p-3">8</td><td class="p-3">Year: 8 = 1968, 9 = 1969, 0 = 1970, 1 = 1971</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">7</td><td class="p-3">S</td><td class="p-3">St. Louis, Missouri</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">8–13</td><td class="p-3">400001</td><td class="p-3">Sequence (1968 began at 400001, 1969 at 700001, 1970 at 400001, 1971 at 100001)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>These VINs don’t name the engine. On 1968–71 cars the engine is identified by the suffix code stamped on the block, next to the partial VIN.</p>
<h2>1972–80: engine added to the VIN</h2>
<p>From 1972 the layout became, for example, <strong>1Z37L6S400001</strong>: 1 = Chevrolet, Z = Corvette, 37 = coupe (67 = convertible; 87 used on 1978–80 coupes), engine character, year (2–9 for 1972–79, A for 1980), S = St. Louis, then the sequence.</p>
<h2>Engine codes by year</h2>
<div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-6"><table class="w-full text-sm text-left text-silver-300 border border-white/10"><thead class="bg-white/5 text-silver-200"><tr><th class="p-3">Year</th><th class="p-3">Engine character</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">1972</td><td class="p-3">K = 350 base (200 hp), L = LT1 (255 hp), W = LS5 454 (270 hp)</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">1973</td><td class="p-3">J = L48 350 (190 hp), T = L82 (250 hp), Z = LS4 454 (275 hp)</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">1974</td><td class="p-3">J = L48 (195 hp), T = L82 (250 hp), Z = LS4 454 (270 hp)</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">1975</td><td class="p-3">J = L48 (165 hp), T = L82 (205 hp)</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">1976–77</td><td class="p-3">L = L48 (180 hp), X = L82 (210 hp)</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">1978</td><td class="p-3">L = L48 (175 hp), X = L82 (220 hp)</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">1979</td><td class="p-3">8 = L48 (195 hp), 4 = L82 (225 hp)</td></tr><tr class="border-t border-white/10"><td class="p-3">1980</td><td class="p-3">H = LG4 305 (180 hp), 8 = L48 (190 hp), 6 = L82 (230 hp)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
<p>The same engine wore different letters in different years. The L82 was T, then X, then 4, then 6. Always read position 5 against your car’s year.</p>
<h2>Numbers-matching checks</h2>
<p>Match the windshield-pillar VIN plate to the engine pad’s partial VIN and suffix, the trim tag and the documentation. Big-block (LS4/LS5) and LT1 cars bring premiums and are worth extra scrutiny.</p>
<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2><h3>Does a 1968 Corvette VIN show the engine?</h3><p>No. 1968–71 Corvette VINs only identify a V8; the specific engine comes from the suffix code stamped on the block.</p><h3>What does 1Z37 mean on a Corvette VIN?</h3><p>1 is Chevrolet, Z is Corvette and 37 is the coupe body style used on 1972–80 C3 VINs.</p><h3>What year is a Corvette VIN with A in the sixth position?</h3><p>On 1972–80 Corvettes the sixth character A means 1980.</p>
